cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this repository or its results, please cite the work below."
type: software
title: "Reconstruction-independent moment tests for sample-based spectral functions"
authors:
  - family-names: "Bonilla Vargas"
    given-names: "Nicolás"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0006-6155-4391"
    affiliation: "Universidad Nacional de Colombia, Bogotá, Colombia; SRH University, Munich, Germany; Daita AI, Munich, Germany"
    email: "ngbonillav@unal.edu.co"
repository-code: "https://github.com/nicolasbonilla/reconstruction-independent-moment-tests"
url: "https://github.com/nicolasbonilla/reconstruction-independent-moment-tests"
version: "2.0.0"
date-released: "2026-10-02"
abstract: >-
  Code and data for "Reconstruction-independent moment tests for sample-based spectral functions". How can
  a spectral function A(ω) from sample-based quantum diagonalization be tested without trusting it? Not
  with its own moments, which agree with it by construction, but with full-operator moments <J†(H-e0)^k J>
  of the probe J on the subspace ground state, of energy e0. We prove that the two differ exactly by the
  probe state's leak out of the subspace: the missed weight at k=0, and zero through order 2K+1 when the
  subspace holds the probe state's order-K Krylov space. Off an eigenstate the operator form matters: a
  lone commutator-form m1 check can pass a first moment 11% low. The m1 and m2 checks detect weight moved
  in frequency at fixed total weight, which captured weight cannot see. Scored against a numerical
  tolerance, this deterministic leak flags almost every sampled support and does not track the error, so
  detection and false-positive rates use exact moments plus shot noise. In a pre-registered, hash-sealed
  blinded simulation (300 instances) on a doped Hubbard ring, the m0-m2 test rejected all 56 truncations,
  20 of 34 weight-preserving spurious features and none of 71 under-converged Krylov spectra. Its
  false-positive rate was 1/112, or 40/112 under the pre-registered 1.96-fold larger noise. No m0-m2 check
  is guaranteed to detect weight moved among four or more fixed poles, and full-support Rayleigh-Ritz
  passes the default thresholds with relative L1 error 0.93. On IBM Heron (ibm_fez) samples, with all
  moments computed classically, captured weight rewards support spreading, not fidelity. The repository
  holds the exact sector-Lanczos/Haydock engine, the generators behind the quoted numbers and figures, the
  sealed records, the raw counts and reference samplers of the device run, and the record of the sealed
  on-device n_k test (not run; no job submitted).
